WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.228 --> 00:00:04.287
- Good morning, Believe Nation!

00:00:04.287 --> 00:00:05.335
I'm Evan Carmichael!

00:00:05.335 --> 00:00:06.995
My one word is believe,

00:00:06.995 --> 00:00:09.433
and I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:12.025
all of the world's major problems!

00:00:12.025 --> 00:00:13.633
I started the Believe Life Series,

00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:16.716
to try to step outside just
the world of entrepreneurship,

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.402
and learn from different areas,

00:00:18.402 --> 00:00:20.593
to try to help us become better,

00:00:20.593 --> 00:00:22.552
more well-rounded individuals,

00:00:22.552 --> 00:00:24.694
who can go out, and have a big impact.

00:00:24.694 --> 00:00:25.612
So, in today's episode,

00:00:25.612 --> 00:00:28.804
we're going to learn how you
can learn a new language.

00:00:28.804 --> 00:00:30.714
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:30.714 --> 00:00:33.143
and I'd love to know one
you guys like the best.

00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:34.632
And as always, guys, as you're watching,

00:00:34.632 --> 00:00:37.433
if someone says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:38.812
please leave it down,
in the comments below,

00:00:38.812 --> 00:00:39.822
and put quotes around it,

00:00:39.822 --> 00:00:41.532
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:41.532 --> 00:00:43.343
And when you write it down for yourself,

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:46.904
it's much more likely to
stick, for you, as well.

00:00:46.904 --> 00:00:47.752
Enjoy.

00:00:47.752 --> 00:00:51.085
(light uplifting music)

00:01:04.865 --> 00:01:06.549
- People did not know about me,

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:09.701
is people see learning from tourists,

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:12.699
but, thinks there's no way to
learn English at that time.

00:01:12.699 --> 00:01:14.525
There is no teachers in our city,

00:01:14.525 --> 00:01:17.482
who can teach people to speak English!

00:01:17.482 --> 00:01:21.693
So, I listen to the BBC,
and Voice of America.

00:01:21.693 --> 00:01:23.783
And there, every evening,

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:26.294
nine o'clock, or eight
o'clock, to nine o'clock,

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:27.596
I open my video.

00:01:27.596 --> 00:01:31.820
From the first Monday,
Wednesday, and Friday,

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.428
I listen to the VOA.

00:01:33.428 --> 00:01:34.969
The first book I heard about,

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:37.719
is The Adventure of Tom Sawyer's.

00:01:39.419 --> 00:01:41.169
And sometimes, if the signal is better,

00:01:41.169 --> 00:01:44.678
I can listen to the
BBC English World News.

00:01:44.678 --> 00:01:47.189
So, by listening to them,

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:49.691
and there's English programs
for those two radios.

00:01:49.691 --> 00:01:51.989
So, this struck me, that today,

00:01:51.989 --> 00:01:55.139
there's so many kids, in the
rural areas, in the poor areas.

00:01:55.139 --> 00:01:56.189
There is no education.

00:01:56.189 --> 00:01:59.040
If we can use that types of radio,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.439
how about using the Internet,
your mobile phone, iPad.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:06.843
It's much more fancier, and more,

00:02:06.843 --> 00:02:09.349
that can give the kids a
much better opportunity.

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:11.020
- Either, you have a favorite TV show.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.620
You have a favorite Netflix series.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.453
And the best part is,

00:02:13.453 --> 00:02:15.168
you can probably find those equivalents

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:17.241
in foreign languages, people!

00:02:17.241 --> 00:02:21.008
- Or, I'd recommend cooking
shows, House Hunters, The Voice,

00:02:21.008 --> 00:02:22.476
these things that you
don't really need to have

00:02:22.476 --> 00:02:24.174
a high level of language, to follow along.

00:02:24.174 --> 00:02:25.393
- But, you can still follow along.

00:02:25.393 --> 00:02:27.172
Like, smiles and expressions.

00:02:27.172 --> 00:02:28.651
Oh, it's French!
(group laughing)

00:02:28.651 --> 00:02:30.588
You always watch that
French House Hunters show!

00:02:30.588 --> 00:02:31.868
- You know what I love
more than House Hunters?

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:32.701
House Hunters International.

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:33.708
But, you know what I love even more,

00:02:33.708 --> 00:02:34.939
than House Hunters International?

00:02:34.939 --> 00:02:36.149
- House Hunters International, in French!

00:02:36.149 --> 00:02:36.982
- Exactly!

00:02:36.982 --> 00:02:38.944
It's called Recherche
appartement ou maison.

00:02:38.944 --> 00:02:39.995
- It's weird, I never watch TV.

00:02:39.995 --> 00:02:41.826
But, when I do, it's usually cartoons.

00:02:41.826 --> 00:02:43.646
And I watch different language versions

00:02:43.646 --> 00:02:44.804
of cartoons I already know.

00:02:44.804 --> 00:02:46.677
So, I watch an episode of Hey Arnold.

00:02:46.677 --> 00:02:48.215
Hey Arnold, in French,

00:02:48.215 --> 00:02:50.444
because I know what happened,
in English, in that episode.

00:02:50.444 --> 00:02:51.695
- It's not like you're completely lost.

00:02:51.695 --> 00:02:54.566
And if you were to watch a
crime, scandalous series,

00:02:54.566 --> 00:02:55.625
you're not going to get that.

00:02:55.625 --> 00:02:57.778
- I don't even understand that in English!

00:02:57.778 --> 00:02:59.108
I honestly don't!

00:02:59.108 --> 00:03:01.894
- The second thing, which
I find really important,

00:03:01.894 --> 00:03:03.633
to learn a language, is believe.

00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:04.855
Believe, believe, believe.

00:03:04.855 --> 00:03:07.758
Because, if you do not have this base,

00:03:07.758 --> 00:03:10.392
it won't go anywhere, I tell you.

00:03:10.392 --> 00:03:11.313
So, believe.

00:03:11.313 --> 00:03:14.183
Believe that, in
yourself, in your ability,

00:03:14.183 --> 00:03:15.324
that you can learn it.

00:03:15.324 --> 00:03:17.782
Because, if you don't
believe it from the start,

00:03:17.782 --> 00:03:20.537
you will not be successful in learning.

00:03:20.537 --> 00:03:22.876
If you're, let's say,
starting out with Spanish,

00:03:22.876 --> 00:03:26.874
and you're saying, oh my
gosh, this is so difficult,

00:03:26.874 --> 00:03:28.263
you're already failing,

00:03:28.263 --> 00:03:30.553
because, you're speaking
the words, already,

00:03:30.553 --> 00:03:32.210
that you won't succeed.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:33.232
And this is so true,

00:03:33.232 --> 00:03:36.320
because, I see it when people
start off, for example,

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.153
German.

00:03:37.153 --> 00:03:39.298
German, okay, it's a difficult language.

00:03:39.298 --> 00:03:43.819
But, if you are like, oh
my gosh, it's so difficult,

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:45.009
I'm never going to learn it.

00:03:45.009 --> 00:03:47.629
I hear so many people saying that.

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:49.720
Even if they're really interested

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.458
into learning the language,

00:03:51.458 --> 00:03:53.728
but, they're saying those words,

00:03:53.728 --> 00:03:57.049
like, I don't know why you even start.

00:03:57.049 --> 00:03:59.159
Because, if you don't have self-belief,

00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:04.090
that you can do it, even
though it's difficult, then...

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.138
Where is the point in starting?

00:04:06.138 --> 00:04:10.040
And there's even studies
that prove the people,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.110
like polyglots, for example,

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.711
and people who have a harder
time learning languages,

00:04:15.711 --> 00:04:17.128
the main difference between them,

00:04:17.128 --> 00:04:19.438
is basically that the people,

00:04:19.438 --> 00:04:22.170
who have the ability to
learn many languages,

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.261
they are motivated,
and also, they believe.

00:04:25.261 --> 00:04:27.359
They believe in themselves,
and their ability,

00:04:27.359 --> 00:04:29.868
that they can do it, actually.

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:32.292
So, if I go on, and for example,

00:04:32.292 --> 00:04:33.754
like my example with Portuguese,

00:04:33.754 --> 00:04:35.736
I started learning Portuguese by myself.

00:04:35.736 --> 00:04:37.896
And I had the belief, like no moment,

00:04:37.896 --> 00:04:39.994
I was like, no, I'm never
going to learn this.

00:04:39.994 --> 00:04:41.805
I'm never going to be fluent.

00:04:41.805 --> 00:04:44.990
And from the beginning, I was like, okay,

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:48.186
I want to learn this, I'm
going to learn it, and I will.

00:04:48.186 --> 00:04:49.091
And that's it.

00:04:49.091 --> 00:04:50.013
And I learned it.

00:04:50.013 --> 00:04:53.513
(Matthew speaking German)

00:06:14.422 --> 00:06:18.005
(Matthew speaking Spanish)

00:06:41.962 --> 00:06:42.795
- Many people say that

00:06:42.795 --> 00:06:44.350
you should switch your
mobile phone settings

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:47.000
to a foreign language, to get used to it.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.447
But, I actually don't
consider this a good advice.

00:06:49.447 --> 00:06:50.898
At least, not for status.

00:06:50.898 --> 00:06:53.519
I mean, everything in your
cellphone is pretty technical.

00:06:53.519 --> 00:06:54.938
And a thing that you want to learn first,

00:06:54.938 --> 00:06:56.246
how to talk to people,

00:06:56.246 --> 00:06:58.476
before you learn that
Bildschirmeinstellungen

00:06:58.476 --> 00:07:00.125
means Display Settings.

00:07:00.125 --> 00:07:01.843
(Trixi speaking German)

00:07:01.843 --> 00:07:04.534
Bildschirmeinstellungen!

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:06.998
These long, complicated
words, may discourage you.

00:07:06.998 --> 00:07:09.995
So, my advice would be, do
the same language switch thing

00:07:09.995 --> 00:07:11.711
with a game you play.

00:07:11.711 --> 00:07:14.202
This doesn't work for any
kind of game, of course.

00:07:14.202 --> 00:07:15.653
If you would switch, for example,

00:07:15.653 --> 00:07:18.500
Skyrim, to German, you
will learn words like,

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.081
Orkischer Zweihander Mugdigkeit!

00:07:21.081 --> 00:07:23.612
Which is the Orcish
Greatsword of Rariness.

00:07:23.612 --> 00:07:25.730
And it is pretty awesome to know that.

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:29.231
But, when would you ever
need this in a conversation?

00:07:29.231 --> 00:07:32.874
(Trixi speaking German)

00:07:32.874 --> 00:07:35.323
Orkischer Zweihander Mugdigkeit!

00:07:35.323 --> 00:07:36.432
Aw, she's so cute!

00:07:36.432 --> 00:07:37.561
So, if you choose a game,

00:07:37.561 --> 00:07:39.262
make sure it plays in our modern time.

00:07:39.262 --> 00:07:41.801
Or, it at least, uses our modern language.

00:07:41.801 --> 00:07:43.653
A point and click adventure, for example.

00:07:43.653 --> 00:07:44.652
Maybe, one that you like.

00:07:44.652 --> 00:07:45.485
Monkey Island!

00:07:45.485 --> 00:07:48.082
You cannot Monkey Island often enough!

00:07:48.082 --> 00:07:49.751
Or, for example, The Sims,

00:07:49.751 --> 00:07:51.911
to get into contact with
normal life happenings.

00:07:51.911 --> 00:07:53.805
There, you encounter
daily life vocabulary,

00:07:53.805 --> 00:07:55.610
in its natural context,

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.269
which makes it easier to remember,

00:07:57.269 --> 00:07:59.887
and also easier to guess
what it could mean.

00:07:59.887 --> 00:08:02.778
Just make sure you don't
end up speaking Simlish.

00:08:02.778 --> 00:08:04.679
(Trixi speaking Simlish)

00:08:04.679 --> 00:08:07.358
Again, write everything down,
that you consider helpful.

00:08:07.358 --> 00:08:09.657
To easily learn a first set of vocabulary,

00:08:09.657 --> 00:08:12.348
that will come in handy,
for normal conversations,

00:08:12.348 --> 00:08:13.889
buy sticky notes.

00:08:13.889 --> 00:08:15.308
Write the German, and English words,

00:08:15.308 --> 00:08:17.888
for everything in your
flat, or house, on them,

00:08:17.888 --> 00:08:21.029
and pin each of them onto
the item it refers to.

00:08:21.029 --> 00:08:22.109
Of course, not in fashion,

00:08:22.109 --> 00:08:23.778
that they will come off too easily,

00:08:23.778 --> 00:08:25.699
or, are always in the way.

00:08:25.699 --> 00:08:26.547
If you have a pet,

00:08:26.547 --> 00:08:29.728
that's not an appropriate
item for this method.

00:08:29.728 --> 00:08:33.138
And also, for example, for
the mouse of your computer,

00:08:33.138 --> 00:08:34.766
cups, or forks.

00:08:34.766 --> 00:08:36.949
This is just not possible.

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:39.436
So, find things that this
trick actually works with.

00:08:39.436 --> 00:08:41.927
It helps you with seeing the
word, over and over again.

00:08:41.927 --> 00:08:44.818
And to learn it, and memorize
it, without much effort.

00:08:44.818 --> 00:08:46.318
For the other everyday items,

00:08:46.318 --> 00:08:48.576
you can craft little
flashcards with pictures,

00:08:48.576 --> 00:08:49.987
like these I did.

00:08:49.987 --> 00:08:51.488
And whenever you have some free minutes,

00:08:51.488 --> 00:08:53.267
just grab one, and learn with it.

00:08:53.267 --> 00:08:54.379
Talking about vocabulary,

00:08:54.379 --> 00:08:55.609
don't just write things down,

00:08:55.609 --> 00:08:57.737
and try to hammer the words in your head.

00:08:57.737 --> 00:08:59.409
The better, and funnier way,

00:08:59.409 --> 00:09:01.359
is to invent little stories.

00:09:01.359 --> 00:09:04.917
For instance, the cow,
means die Kuh, in German.

00:09:04.917 --> 00:09:07.269
If you're going to
remember, well, try that.

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:10.195
How is the animal called, in English?

00:09:10.195 --> 00:09:11.028
Cow?

00:09:11.028 --> 00:09:13.611
And what sound does a cow make?

00:09:14.888 --> 00:09:15.938
Moo?

00:09:15.938 --> 00:09:17.056
Exactly!

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:19.733
And now, just put these two together.

00:09:19.733 --> 00:09:22.527
(exclaims) Kuh?

00:09:22.527 --> 00:09:23.467
(Trixi gasping)

00:09:23.467 --> 00:09:26.149
Mind... (imitates explosions)

00:09:26.149 --> 00:09:27.028
Blown.

00:09:27.028 --> 00:09:29.208
Of course, this doesn't
work for every single word.

00:09:29.208 --> 00:09:31.600
But, just give your imagination a try.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:34.099
- [Tim] Languages, Material vs. Method.

00:09:34.099 --> 00:09:35.919
I, like many people,
came with the conclusion,

00:09:35.919 --> 00:09:38.180
that I was terrible at languages!

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.499
I suffered through
Spanish, for junior high,

00:09:40.499 --> 00:09:41.808
first year of high school,

00:09:41.808 --> 00:09:44.295
and the sum total of my
knowledge was, pretty much...

00:09:44.295 --> 00:09:45.787
(Tim speaking Spanish)
(audience laughing)

00:09:45.787 --> 00:09:49.583
And I wouldn't even catch the response.

00:09:49.583 --> 00:09:51.003
Sad state of affairs.

00:09:51.003 --> 00:09:54.352
Then, I transferred to a
different school, sophomore year,

00:09:54.352 --> 00:09:55.702
and I had a choice of other languages.

00:09:55.702 --> 00:09:57.243
And most of my friends
were taking Japanese.

00:09:57.243 --> 00:09:58.694
So, I thought, why not punish myself?

00:09:58.694 --> 00:10:00.674
I'll do Japanese.

00:10:00.674 --> 00:10:03.713
Six months later, I had
the chance to go to Japan.

00:10:03.713 --> 00:10:05.303
And my teachers assured me.

00:10:05.303 --> 00:10:06.136
They said, "Don't worry.

00:10:06.136 --> 00:10:08.355
"You'll have Japanese
language classes, every day,

00:10:08.355 --> 00:10:09.668
"to help you cope.

00:10:09.668 --> 00:10:10.568
"It'd be an amazing experience."

00:10:10.568 --> 00:10:14.129
My first overseas experience, in fact.

00:10:14.129 --> 00:10:16.431
So, my parents encouraged me to do it.

00:10:16.431 --> 00:10:17.264
I left.

00:10:17.264 --> 00:10:19.159
I arrived in Tokyo, amazing.

00:10:19.159 --> 00:10:21.159
Couldn't believe I was on
the other side of the world.

00:10:21.159 --> 00:10:23.109
Met my host family, things
went quite well, I think,

00:10:23.109 --> 00:10:24.740
all things considered.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.240
My first evening, before
my first day of school,

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.052
I said to my mother, very politely,

00:10:30.052 --> 00:10:31.802
please wake me up, at 8:00 AM.

00:10:31.802 --> 00:10:33.541
So...
(Tim speaking Japanese)

00:10:33.541 --> 00:10:34.813
But, I didn't say (speaking in Japanese),

00:10:34.813 --> 00:10:37.842
I said (speaking in
Japanese), pretty close.

00:10:37.842 --> 00:10:40.593
But, I said, please rape me, at 8:00 AM.

00:10:40.593 --> 00:10:43.252
(audience laughing)

00:10:43.252 --> 00:10:45.762
You've never seen a more
confused Japanese woman.

00:10:45.762 --> 00:10:47.712
(audience laughing)

00:10:47.712 --> 00:10:49.487
(Tim chuckling)

00:10:49.487 --> 00:10:51.419
And I walked into school.

00:10:51.419 --> 00:10:54.948
And a teacher came up to me,
and handed me a piece of paper,

00:10:54.948 --> 00:10:56.016
and I couldn't read any of it.

00:10:56.016 --> 00:10:57.206
Hieroglyphics.

00:10:57.206 --> 00:10:58.858
It could've been, because it was kanji.

00:10:58.858 --> 00:11:02.117
So, Chinese characters, adapted
into the Japanese language.

00:11:02.117 --> 00:11:03.798
I asked him what this said.

00:11:03.798 --> 00:11:06.277
And he goes, "Ah, okay, okay."

00:11:06.277 --> 00:11:09.610
(Tim speaking Japanese)

00:11:12.669 --> 00:11:13.502
And, so on.

00:11:13.502 --> 00:11:17.245
And it came to me, in waves.
(audience laughing)

00:11:17.245 --> 00:11:19.883
There had been something
lost in translation.

00:11:19.883 --> 00:11:22.815
The Japanese classes, were not
Japanese instruction classes,

00:11:22.815 --> 00:11:23.648
per say.

00:11:23.648 --> 00:11:24.974
They were the normal
high school curriculum

00:11:24.974 --> 00:11:26.402
for Japanese students.

00:11:26.402 --> 00:11:28.843
The other 4,999 students in the school,

00:11:28.843 --> 00:11:30.793
who were Japanese, besides the American.

00:11:30.793 --> 00:11:33.362
And, (exhales) that's
pretty much my response.

00:11:33.362 --> 00:11:35.106
(audience laughing)

00:11:35.106 --> 00:11:38.348
And that set me on this
panic-driven search

00:11:38.348 --> 00:11:40.058
for the perfect language method.

00:11:40.058 --> 00:11:40.891
I tried everything.

00:11:40.891 --> 00:11:41.747
I went to Kinokuniya.

00:11:41.747 --> 00:11:44.516
I tried every possible
book, every possible CD.

00:11:44.516 --> 00:11:46.769
Nothing worked, until I found this.

00:11:46.769 --> 00:11:48.176
This is the Joyo kanji hyo.

00:11:48.176 --> 00:11:51.587
This is a tablet, rather, or a poster,

00:11:51.587 --> 00:11:54.957
of the 1,945 common use characters,

00:11:54.957 --> 00:11:58.036
as determined by the Ministry
of Education, in 1981.

00:11:58.036 --> 00:11:59.888
Many of the publications in Japan,

00:11:59.888 --> 00:12:01.307
limit themselves to these characters,

00:12:01.307 --> 00:12:04.256
to facilitate literacy,
and some were required to.

00:12:04.256 --> 00:12:07.009
And this became my holy
grail, my Rosetta Stone.

00:12:07.009 --> 00:12:11.176
And as soon as I focused on
this material, I took off.

00:12:12.543 --> 00:12:15.454
And I ended up being able
to read Asahi Shinbun,

00:12:15.454 --> 00:12:18.313
Asahi newspaper, about six months later.

00:12:18.313 --> 00:12:19.814
So, a total of 11 months later.

00:12:19.814 --> 00:12:21.454
And went, from Japanese I, to Japanese VI.

00:12:21.454 --> 00:12:23.123
Ended up doing translation
work, at age 16,

00:12:23.123 --> 00:12:24.693
when I returned to the US.

00:12:24.693 --> 00:12:25.526
And...

00:12:27.093 --> 00:12:30.753
Have continued to apply this
material over method approach,

00:12:30.753 --> 00:12:32.533
to close to a dozen languages now.

00:12:32.533 --> 00:12:34.933
Someone who was terrible at languages.

00:12:34.933 --> 00:12:36.474
(chuckles) And any given time,

00:12:36.474 --> 00:12:39.474
speak, read, and write, five or six.

00:12:40.333 --> 00:12:43.196
(chuckles) And this brings
us to the point, which is,

00:12:43.196 --> 00:12:46.235
it's often times, what
you do, not how you do it,

00:12:46.235 --> 00:12:47.867
that is the determining factor.

00:12:47.867 --> 00:12:50.014
This is the difference
between being effective,

00:12:50.014 --> 00:12:52.035
doing the right things,
and being efficient.

00:12:52.035 --> 00:12:54.307
Doing things well, whether,
or not they're important.

00:12:54.307 --> 00:12:55.654
And you can also do this with grammar.

00:12:55.654 --> 00:12:57.665
I came up with these six sentences,

00:12:57.665 --> 00:12:59.404
after much experimentation.

00:12:59.404 --> 00:13:00.847
Having a native speaker allow you

00:13:00.847 --> 00:13:02.475
to deconstruct their grammar,

00:13:02.475 --> 00:13:03.926
by translating these sentences,

00:13:03.926 --> 00:13:05.505
into past, present, future,

00:13:05.505 --> 00:13:07.595
will show you the subject, object, verb,

00:13:07.595 --> 00:13:10.216
placement of indirect/direct
objects, gender, and so forth.

00:13:10.216 --> 00:13:11.606
From that point, you can then,

00:13:11.606 --> 00:13:13.414
if you want to acquire multiple languages,

00:13:13.414 --> 00:13:15.144
alternate them, so
there's no interference.

00:13:15.144 --> 00:13:17.333
And we can talk about that,
if anyone's interested.

00:13:17.333 --> 00:13:19.500
And now, I love languages.

00:13:21.852 --> 00:13:23.042
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:13:23.042 --> 00:13:25.512
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video,

00:13:25.512 --> 00:13:26.943
what was the biggest takeaway

00:13:26.943 --> 00:13:29.192
that you're going to immediately apply,

00:13:29.192 --> 00:13:30.623
somehow in your life.

00:13:30.623 --> 00:13:32.132
Or, maybe your business.

00:13:32.132 --> 00:13:32.965
Let me know!

00:13:32.965 --> 00:13:34.054
Leave it down, in the comments below!

00:13:34.054 --> 00:13:35.142
I'm really curious to find out.

00:13:35.142 --> 00:13:37.223
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Bo Hawkins,

00:13:37.223 --> 00:13:39.183
from 4minutemasters.com!

00:13:39.183 --> 00:13:40.245
Bo, thank you so much,

00:13:40.245 --> 00:13:42.535
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word,

00:13:42.535 --> 00:13:44.616
and doing that fun interview together!

00:13:44.616 --> 00:13:46.236
I really appreciate the support, man.

00:13:46.236 --> 00:13:48.468
And I'm hoping you are enjoying the read.

00:13:48.468 --> 00:13:49.716
So, thank you guys so much for watching!

00:13:49.716 --> 00:13:51.367
I hope you have an amazing day!

00:13:51.367 --> 00:13:52.525
I believe in you.

00:13:52.525 --> 00:13:54.566
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself!

00:13:54.566 --> 00:13:58.255
And whatever your one word is, much love.

00:13:58.255 --> 00:13:59.755
I'll see you soon.

00:14:05.427 --> 00:14:07.116
- See, I always thought I had this thing,

00:14:07.116 --> 00:14:11.226
that was a little bit of
Sid craziness, that I did.

00:14:11.226 --> 00:14:13.446
And then, I realized how useful it was.

00:14:13.446 --> 00:14:17.613
I always did, what I like to
call, shower conversations.

00:14:18.447 --> 00:14:19.696
(audience laughing)

00:14:19.696 --> 00:14:23.266
And shower conversations are
exactly what they sound like.

00:14:23.266 --> 00:14:25.036
When I was learning a new language,

00:14:25.036 --> 00:14:27.326
I would stay in the
shower, for a few minutes,

00:14:27.326 --> 00:14:29.715
and I remember having
all these discussions!

00:14:29.715 --> 00:14:31.343
I remember, when I was learning Chinese,

00:14:31.343 --> 00:14:34.888
and I would haggle, and
try get two yuan more,

00:14:34.888 --> 00:14:38.497
to get that wonderful dumpling,
and getting the discount.

00:14:38.497 --> 00:14:41.408
Or, I would go to Roma, and
I would ask for directions,

00:14:41.408 --> 00:14:43.748
to the best piazza!

00:14:43.748 --> 00:14:44.990
It was amazing.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:47.228
And the beautiful thing
about a shower conversation,

00:14:47.228 --> 00:14:49.138
is that it allows you to find,

00:14:49.138 --> 00:14:51.637
wherever you have a
gap, in your knowledge.

00:14:51.637 --> 00:14:54.977
Because, you're having the
conversation on both ends.

00:14:54.977 --> 00:14:57.328
For example, it's easy
to ask for directions.

00:14:57.328 --> 00:14:59.108
How about receiving them?

00:14:59.108 --> 00:15:01.348
Or, even better, giving directions.

00:15:01.348 --> 00:15:03.348
Well, the shower conversation
forces you to have

00:15:03.348 --> 00:15:05.357
both sides of the conversation.

00:15:05.357 --> 00:15:08.076
And you don't need to
have them in the shower!

00:15:08.076 --> 00:15:09.678
The wonderful thing about them as well,

00:15:09.678 --> 00:15:11.037
is that you can have them anywhere!

00:15:11.037 --> 00:15:13.257
So, you can have them in the
shower, in your apartment,

00:15:13.257 --> 00:15:16.392
walking down the streets, in the subway.

00:15:16.392 --> 00:15:18.876
And seriously, if you're in the subway,

00:15:18.876 --> 00:15:21.309
speaking to yourself, in a
foreign language, in New York.

00:15:21.309 --> 00:15:22.177
You'll fit right in.

00:15:22.177 --> 00:15:24.388
You're fine!
(audience laughing)

00:15:24.388 --> 00:15:26.466
And it's great, because it doesn't,

00:15:26.466 --> 00:15:28.658
you don't depend on anything, or anyone,

00:15:28.658 --> 00:15:30.408
to get your practice.

00:15:30.408 --> 00:15:32.028
And I did this for years.

00:15:32.028 --> 00:15:33.017
And later on, I found out,

00:15:33.017 --> 00:15:34.817
that professional athletes to do it, too!

00:15:34.817 --> 00:15:37.458
Michael Phelps is known to visualize

00:15:37.458 --> 00:15:40.198
every single one of his
races, several times over,

00:15:40.198 --> 00:15:42.405
before jumping in the water!

00:15:42.405 --> 00:15:43.748
Worked great for him!

00:15:43.748 --> 00:15:45.600
And it works great for me, too.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:48.168
So, it would work for you as well.

00:15:48.168 --> 00:15:50.769
- Now, when it comes
to learning a language,

00:15:50.769 --> 00:15:54.618
perhaps, the most
crucial element, is time.

00:15:54.618 --> 00:15:58.057
And by time, I don't
mean years, upon years,

00:15:58.057 --> 00:16:01.949
of endless learning, as some
people still like to think.

00:16:01.949 --> 00:16:05.958
How long does it take,
to learn a language?

00:16:05.958 --> 00:16:07.728
How about, if I were to tell you,

00:16:07.728 --> 00:16:11.895
that 30 minutes, per day, a
great and effective start.

00:16:14.059 --> 00:16:17.278
Now, 30 minutes, these are
minutes that we all have.

00:16:17.278 --> 00:16:19.959
Be it ten in the morning,
ten in the afternoon,

00:16:19.959 --> 00:16:23.698
ten in the evening, or 30
minutes, in simply one go.

00:16:23.698 --> 00:16:27.407
On the way to work, to
university, to school,

00:16:27.407 --> 00:16:28.769
out in the evening, meeting friends,

00:16:28.769 --> 00:16:30.977
whilst on the train, or a bus.

00:16:30.977 --> 00:16:35.079
We all have these minutes,
that we can commit to learn.

00:16:35.079 --> 00:16:38.637
Furthermore, by learning,
for small periods,

00:16:38.637 --> 00:16:40.648
and at regular intervals,

00:16:40.648 --> 00:16:44.059
we won't feel so
overwhelmed by the language.

00:16:44.059 --> 00:16:48.578
And, even better, learning
for regular periods,

00:16:48.578 --> 00:16:50.999
means that it's more effective.

00:16:50.999 --> 00:16:52.107
Because, chances are,

00:16:52.107 --> 00:16:54.659
that if you're learning for once a week,

00:16:54.659 --> 00:16:55.991
or once a fortnight,

00:16:55.991 --> 00:16:58.168
by the time you next come to learn,

00:16:58.168 --> 00:17:02.138
you'll already have forgotten
what you initially learned.

00:17:02.138 --> 00:17:03.767
The goal, therefore,

00:17:03.767 --> 00:17:07.729
is to fit language learning
into our daily routines,

00:17:07.729 --> 00:17:10.516
and not the other way around.

00:17:10.516 --> 00:17:13.378
And by doing this, there's no reason why,

00:17:13.378 --> 00:17:15.296
after simply one month,

00:17:15.296 --> 00:17:18.138
you can't get by in your new language.

00:17:18.138 --> 00:17:20.702
- [Chris] A third action, start mixing!

00:17:20.702 --> 00:17:22.178
You probably have never thought of this!

00:17:22.178 --> 00:17:25.179
But, if you got ten verbs,
ten nouns, and ten adjectives,

00:17:25.179 --> 00:17:28.096
you can say 1,000 different things!

00:17:29.028 --> 00:17:32.087
Right, language is a creative process!

00:17:32.087 --> 00:17:34.078
What do babies do?

00:17:34.078 --> 00:17:36.386
Okay, me, ba, now!

00:17:36.386 --> 00:17:38.026
Okay, that's how they communicate!

00:17:38.026 --> 00:17:40.687
So, start mixing, get
creative, have fun with it!

00:17:40.687 --> 00:17:41.636
It doesn't have to be perfect!

00:17:41.636 --> 00:17:44.028
It just has to work!

00:17:44.028 --> 00:17:47.284
And when you're doing this,
you focus on the core!

00:17:47.284 --> 00:17:48.823
What does that mean?

00:17:48.823 --> 00:17:51.266
Well, with every language,
there's high frequency content!

00:17:51.266 --> 00:17:55.130
In English, 1,000 words covers 85 percent

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.910
of anything you're ever going
to say, in daily communication!

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:00.801
3,000 words gives you 98 percent

00:18:00.801 --> 00:18:03.620
of anything you're going to
say, in daily conversation!

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:05.197
You got 3,000 words!

00:18:05.197 --> 00:18:06.490
You're speakin' the language!

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:09.541
The rest is icing on the cake!

00:18:09.541 --> 00:18:11.831
And when you're just
beginning with a new language,

00:18:11.831 --> 00:18:13.502
start with your tool box!

00:18:13.502 --> 00:18:15.322
Week number one!

00:18:15.322 --> 00:18:17.972
In your new language, you say things like,

00:18:17.972 --> 00:18:18.820
how do you say that?

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:19.670
I don't understand.

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:20.503
Repeat that, please!

00:18:20.503 --> 00:18:21.751
What does that mean?

00:18:21.751 --> 00:18:23.412
All in your target language!

00:18:23.412 --> 00:18:26.662
You're using it as a tool,
making it useful to you!

00:18:26.662 --> 00:18:30.032
It's relevant to learn other
things about the language!

00:18:30.032 --> 00:18:32.990
By week two, you should
be saying things like,

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:36.711
me, this, you, that, give, you know, hot!

00:18:36.711 --> 00:18:39.860
Simple pronouns, simple nouns!

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:41.410
Simple verbs, simple adjectives!

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:42.780
Communicating like a baby!

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.559
And by the third, or fourth week,

00:18:44.559 --> 00:18:48.059
you're getting into
what I call, glue words!

00:18:48.059 --> 00:18:49.905
Although, but, therefore!

00:18:49.905 --> 00:18:52.314
These are logical transformers,

00:18:52.314 --> 00:18:54.530
that tie bits of a language together,

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:57.254
allowing you to make more complex meaning!

00:18:57.254 --> 00:18:59.754
At that point, you're talking!

